n":null,"tei_xml":"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<TEI xml:space=\"preserve\" xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" \nxmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\" \nxsi:schemaLocation=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0 https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kermitt2/grobid/master/grobid-home/schemas/xsd/Grobid.xsd\"\n xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\t<teiHeader xml:lang=\"en\">\n\t\t<fileDesc>\n\t\t\t<titleStmt>\n\t\t\t\t<title level=\"a\" type=\"main\">Web-Bench: A LLM Code Benchmark Based on Web Standards and Frameworks</title>\n\t\t\t\t<funder ref=\"#_MFmMzZ3\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<orgName type=\"full\">unknown</orgName>\n\t\t\t\t</funder>\n\t\t\t</titleStmt>\n\t\t\t<publicationStmt>\n\t\t\t\t<publisher/>\n\t\t\t\t<availability  status=\"unknown\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<licence/>\n\t\t\t\t</availability>\n\t\t\t\t<date type=\"published\" when=\"2025-05-12\">12 May 2025</date>\n\t\t\t</publicationStmt>\n\t\t\t<sourceDesc>\n\t\t\t\t<biblStruct status=\"extracted\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<analytic>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Kai</forename><surname>Xu</surname></persName>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t</author>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Yiwei</forename><surname>Mao</surname></persName>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t</author>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Xinyi</forename><surname>Guan</surname></persName>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t</author>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Zilong</forename><surname>Feng</surname></persName>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t</author>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<title level=\"a\" type=\"main\">Web-Bench: A LLM Code Benchmark Based on Web Standards and Frameworks</title>\n\t\t\t\t\t</analytic>\n\t\t\t\t\t<monogr>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<imprint>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<date type=\"published\" when=\"2025-05-12\">12 May 2025</date>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t</imprint>\n\t\t\t\t\t</monogr>\n\t\t\t\t\t<idno type=\"MD5\">67A32A9E4E538BD5B32AE147727DE2AA</idno>\n\t\t\t\t\t<idno type=\"arXiv\">arXiv:2505.07473v1[cs.AI]</idno>\n\t\t\t\t</biblStruct>\n\t\t\t</sourceDesc>\n\t\t</fileDesc>\n\t\t<encodingDesc>\n\t\t\t<appInfo>\n\t\t\t\t<application version=\"0.9.0\" ident=\"GROBID\" when=\"2026-08-21T15:08+0000\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<desc>GROBID - A machine learning software for extracting information from scholarly documents</desc>\n\t\t\t\t\t<label type=\"revision\">0.9.0</label>\n\t\t\t\t\t<label type=\"parameters\">startPage=-1, endPage=-1, consolidateCitations=0, consolidateHeader=0, consolidateFunders=0, includeRawAffiliations=false, includeRawCitations=false, includeRawCopyrights=false, generateTeiIds=false, generateTeiCoordinates=[[\"figure\",\"table\",\"note\",\"formula\"]], flavor=null</label>\n\t\t\t\t\t<ref target=\"https://github.com/kermitt2/grobid\"/>\n\t\t\t\t</application>\n\t\t\t</appInfo>\n\t\t</encodingDesc>\n\t\t<profileDesc>\n\t\t\t<abstract>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><p>The application of large language models (LLMs) in the field of coding is evolving rapidly: from code assistants, to autonomous coding agents, and then to generating complete projects through natural language. Early LLM code benchmarks primarily focused on code generation accuracy, but these benchmarks have gradually become saturated. Benchmark saturation weakens their guiding role for LLMs. For example, HumanEval Pass@1 has reached 99.4% and MBPP 94.2%. Among various attempts to address benchmark saturation, approaches based on software engineering have stood out, but the saturation of existing software engineering benchmarks is rapidly increasing. To address this, we propose a new benchmark, Web-Bench 12 , which contains 50 projects, each consisting of 20 tasks with sequential dependencies. The tasks implement project features in sequence, simulating real-world human development workflows. When designing Web-Bench, we aim to cover the foundational elements of Web development: Web Standards and Web Frameworks. Given the scale and complexity of these projects, which were designed by engineers with 5 to 10 years of experience, each presents a significant challenge. On average, a single project takes 4 to 8 hours for a senior engineer to complete. On our given benchmark agent (Web-Agent), SOTA (Claude 3.7 Sonnet) achieves only 25.1% Pass@1, significantly lower (better) than SWE-Bench's Verified (65.4%) and Full (33.8%) scores. Finally, we discuss that in any development field, Standards and Frameworks represent foundational knowledge and efficiency tools, respectively, and LLMs require optimization tailored to them.</p></div>\n\t\t\t</abstract>\n\t\t</profileDesc>\n\t</teiHeader>\n\t<facsimile>\n\t\t<surface n=\"1\" ulx=\"0.0\" uly=\"0.0\" lrx=\"595.276\" lry=\"841.89\"/>\n\t\t<surface n=\"2\" ulx=\"0.0\" uly=\"0.0\" lrx=\"595.276\" lry=\"841.89\"/>\n\t\t<surface n=\"3\" ulx=\"0.0\" uly=\"0.0\" lrx=\"595.276\" lry=\"841.89\"/>\n\t\t<surface n=\"4\" ulx=\"0.0\" uly=\"0.0\" lrx=\"595.276\" lry=\"841.89\"/>\n\t\t<surface n=\"5\" ulx=\"0.0\" uly=\"0.0\" lrx=\"595.276\" lry=\"841.89\"/>\n\t\t<surface n=\"6\" ulx=\"0.0\" uly=\"0.0\" lrx=\"595.276\" lry=\"841.89\"/>\n\t\t<surface n=\"7\" ulx=\"0.0\" uly=\"0.0\" lrx=\"595.276\" lry=\"841.89\"/>\n\t\t<surface n=\"8\" ulx=\"0.0\" uly=\"0.0\" lrx=\"595.276\" lry=\"841.89\"/>\n\t\t<surface n=\"9\" ulx=\"0.0\" uly=\"0.0\" lrx=\"595.276\" lry=\"841.89\"/>\n\t\t<surface n=\"10\" ulx=\"0.0\" uly=\"0.0\" lrx=\"595.276\" lry=\"841.89\"/>\n\t\t<surface n=\"11\" ulx=\"0.0\" uly=\"0.0\" lrx=\"595.276\" lry=\"841.89\"/>\n\t\t<surface n=\"12\" ulx=\"0.0\" uly=\"0.0\" lrx=\"595.276\" lry=\"841.89\"/>\n\t\t<surface n=\"13\" ulx=\"0.0\" uly=\"0.0\" lrx=\"595.276\" lry=\"841.89\"/>\n\t\t<surface n=\"14\" ulx=\"0.0\" uly=\"0.0\" lrx=\"595.276\" lry=\"841.89\"/>\n\t\t<surface n=\"15\" ulx=\"0.0\" uly=\"0.0\" lrx=\"595.276\" lry=\"841.89\"/>\n\t\t<surface n=\"16\" ulx=\"0.0\" uly=\"0.0\" lrx=\"595.276\" lry=\"841.89\"/>\n\t\t<surface n=\"17\" ulx=\"0.0\" uly=\"0.0\" lrx=\"595.276\" lry=\"841.89\"/>\n\t\t<surface n=\"18\" ulx=\"0.0\" uly=\"0.0\" lrx=\"595.276\" lry=\"841.89\"/>\n\t\t<surface n=\"19\" ulx=\"0.0\" uly=\"0.0\" lrx=\"595.276\" lry=\"841.89\"/>\n\t\t<surface n=\"20\" ulx=\"0.0\" uly=\"0.0\" lrx=\"595.276\" lry=\"841.89\"/>\n\t\t<surface n=\"21\" ulx=\"0.0\" uly=\"0.0\" lrx=\"595.276\" lry=\"841.89\"/>\n\t\t<surface n=\"22\" ulx=\"0.0\" uly=\"0.0\" lrx=\"595.276\" lry=\"841.89\"/>\n\t\t<surface n=\"23\" ulx=\"0.0\" uly=\"0.0\" lrx=\"595.276\" lry=\"841.89\"/>\n\t\t<surface n=\"24\" ulx=\"0.0\" uly=\"0.0\" lrx=\"595.276\" lry=\"841.89\"/>\n\t\t<surface n=\"25\" ulx=\"0.0\" uly=\"0.0\" lrx=\"595.276\" lry=\"841.89\"/>\n\t\t<surface n=\"26\" ulx=\"0.0\" uly=\"0.0\" lrx=\"595.276\" lry=\"841.89\"/>\n\t\t<surface n=\"27\" ulx=\"0.0\" uly=\"0.0\" lrx=\"595.276\" lry=\"841.89\"/>\n\t\t<surface n=\"28\" ulx=\"0.0\" uly=\"0.0\" lrx=\"595.276\" lry=\"841.89\"/>\n\t</facsimile>\n\t<text xml:lang=\"en\">\n\t\t<body>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head n=\"1.\">Introduction</head><p>The application scope of large language models (LLMs) is constantly expanding and already includes fields such as chatbots, search, and scientific research. Applications in the field of coding are also evolving rapidly, from basic code assistants <ref type=\"bibr\">[1,</ref><ref type=\"bibr\">2]</ref>, to autonomous coding agents <ref type=\"bibr\">[3]</ref>, and then to attempts to generate complete projects through natural language <ref type=\"bibr\">[4,</ref><ref type=\"bibr\">5]</ref>. Although it is still in the early stages of exploration, the potential of LLM coding is already evident despite limited practical results.</p><p>Early LLM code benchmarks focused on the accuracy of code generation, but these benchmarks have gradually become saturated: HumanEval <ref type=\"bibr\">[6]</ref> SOTA 99.4% <ref type=\"bibr\">[7]</ref> and MBPP <ref type=\"bibr\">[8]</ref> SOTA 94.2% <ref type=\"bibr\">[9]</ref>. Benchmark saturation weakens their guiding role for LLMs. To solve this problem, many new ideas have emerged, such as increasing programming languages <ref type=\"bibr\">[10,</ref><ref type=\"bibr\">11,</ref><ref type=\"bibr\" target=\"#b11\">12]</ref>, increasing test coverage <ref type=\"bibr\">[13,</ref><ref type=\"bibr\" target=\"#b13\">14]</ref>, increasing dataset complexity <ref type=\"bibr\">[15,</ref><ref type=\"bibr\" target=\"#b15\">16,</ref><ref type=\"bibr\" target=\"#b16\">17,</ref><ref type=\"bibr\" target=\"#b17\">18]</ref>, and improving software engineering reproducibility <ref type=\"bibr\" target=\"#b18\">[19,</ref><ref type=\"bibr\" target=\"#b19\">20,</ref><ref type=\"bibr\" target=\"#b20\">21,</ref><ref type=\"bibr\" target=\"#b21\">22]</ref>. We prefer to find a method that can measure the actual complexity of codes. This means that the benchmark should cover the coding process as much as possible and be close to real projects in 1 <ref type=\"url\" target=\"https://github.com/bytedance/web-bench\">https://github.com/bytedance/web-bench</ref> 2 <ref type=\"url\" target=\"https://huggingface.co/datasets/bytedance-research/Web-\">https://huggingface.co/datasets/bytedance-research/Web-</ref>Bench terms of logical complexity. Among many ideas, software engineering is undoubtedly the closest to this goal.</p><p>Existing software engineering benchmarks, such as SWE-Bench <ref type=\"bibr\" target=\"#b21\">[22]</ref> and RepoBench <ref type=\"bibr\" target=\"#b19\">[20]</ref>, both use independent tasks to organize datasets. Although the data in these benchmarks may come from real projects, the tasks themselves lack coherence and differ significantly from real-world development workflows. This paper proposes a new benchmark, Web-Bench, which contains 50 projects, each consisting of 20 tasks with sequential dependencies. The tasks implement project features in sequence, simulating real-world human development workflows. When designing Web-Bench, we aim to cover the foundational elements of Web development: Web Standards and Web Frameworks. Web Standards are basic knowledge of Web development and cover CSS Selector, CSS Layout, DOM, SVG, WebGL, ECMAScript <ref type=\"bibr\" target=\"#b22\">[23]</ref>, TypeScript <ref type=\"bibr\" target=\"#b23\">[24]</ref>, etc. Web Frameworks provide higher-level abstraction capability and improve development efficiency, covering key aspects of Web development such as UI Framework <ref type=\"bibr\" target=\"#b24\">[25]</ref>, State Management <ref type=\"bibr\" target=\"#b25\">[26]</ref>, CSS Framework, Fullstack Framework, ORM, Build, etc.</p><p>Given the scale and complexity of these projects, which were designed by engineers with 5-10 years of experience, each presents a significant challenge. On average, a single project takes 4-8 hours for a senior engineer to complete. On our given benchmark agent (Web-Agent), SOTA (Claude 3.7 Sonnet) achieves only 25.1% Pass@1, significantly lower than SWE-Bench's 65.4% (Verified) <ref type=\"bibr\" target=\"#b26\">[27]</ref> and 33.8% (Full). A year ago (2024.4), SWE-Bench (Verified) SOTA was only 25.6% which has increased rapidly. Table <ref type=\"table\" target=\"#tab_2\">3</ref> below compares the SOTA of more benchmarks.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head n=\"2.\">Benchmark Design</head></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head n=\"2.1\">Benchmark Construction</head><p>The Web-Bench dataset contains 50 projects, each consisting of 20 tasks with sequential dependencies. See Figure <ref type=\"figure\">1</ref>.</p><p>1. Dataset: a collection of projects. Due to the evaluation cost, we plan to provide a Lite version in the future.</p><p>2. Project: a single item in the dataset.</p><p>3. Task: including a description and several end-toend (E2E) testcases. On average, there are 3.6 cases per task and 72.4 cases per project, as shown in Table <ref type=\"table\" target=\"#tab_0\">1</ref>.</p><p>Figure <ref type=\"figure\">1</ref>: Task-2 depends on the execution result ('main' element) of Task-1</p><p>During the design process of the project, different business scenarios 2 were selected: Games, Utilities, Charts, Drawing Boards, Surveys, Data Tables, Blog Sites, Document Sites, etc., so that the final Benchmark is closer to real-world software engineering.  Web-Bench covers the core Web standards and mainstream development frameworks 3 4 that Web application development relies on. see Appendix A for more details. 2.2 Standard and Framework 2.2.1 Standard Web Standards are the basic knowledge of Web development, especially those that are frequently used, offer extensive functionality, and enable more complex features. We have selected the core standards that Web applications rely on, covering HTML, CSS and JavaScript. Partial core standards: • ECMAScript: the JavaScript language standard that ensures the interoperability of web pages across different web browsers. • TypeScript: A strongly typed programming language that builds on JavaScript. TypeScript code converts to JavaScript, which runs anywhere JavaScript runs. Most modern web applications are now built with TypeScript.</p><p>• CSS Selector: Patterns that match against elements in a tree and are one of the core technologies used to select nodes in a document.</p><p>• Flexbox: A layout model that arranges items in one dimension. The children of a flex container can be laid out in any direction, and can \"flex\" their sizes, either growing to fill unused space or shrinking to avoid overflowing the parent. It is widely used in most web applications.</p><p>• Grid: A two-dimensional grid-based layout system. The children of a grid container can be positioned into arbitrary slots in a predefined flexible or fixedsize layout grid. It is more powerful than flexbox and also widely used in web applications.</p><p>• DOM (Document Object Model): defines a platform neutral model for events and node trees. It is the main technology used to manipulate web page elements.</p><p>• SVG (Scalable Vector Graphics): An XML-based language for describing two-dimensional graphics. SVG drawings can be interactive and dynamic.</p><p>Animations can be defined and triggered declaratively.</p><p>• Canvas: Used for drawing graphics via JavaScript and the HTML &lt;canvas&gt; element. It can be used for animation, game graphics, data visualization, photo manipulation, and real-time video processing.</p><p>• WebGL (Web Graphic Language): An immediate mode 3D rendering API designed for the web. It is derived from OpenGL® ES 3.0, and provides similar rendering functionality, but in an HTML context.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head n=\"2.2.2\">Framework</head><p>In the early stages of Web development, there were not many development frameworks, and various Web applications could be developed using native technologies. However, with the rise of Web 2.0, both the technical complexity and application scale of Web applications have increased dramatically, and the development efficiency based solely on native Web technology has been challenged. Development frameworks have brought greater abstraction capabilities, improved development efficiency, and enhanced collaboration to Web development, covering its main aspects:</p><p>• UI Framework: Usually provides a componentbased development model and supporting development tools to improve the efficiency of singleunit development and collaboration, such as React, Vue, Angular, Threejs, etc.</p><p>• State Management: UI state management is one of the core tasks in Web application development. Excellent frameworks will simplify the state model and improve development and maintenance efficiency, such as Redux, Zustand, Jotai, Mobx, etc.</p><p>• CSS Framework: Improves CSS maintainability and development efficiency by enhancing basic capabilities such as variables, modules, functions, or providing CSS utilities. Examples include SASS, LESS, Stylus, Tailwind, etc.</p><p>• Build: Web applications eventually run in browsers, and during the development phase, JS code and various resources (CSS, images, icons etc.) are usually split into many modules (or files), which require modular packaging using build tools, as well as optimization such as merging and compression. At the same time, build tools are also required to unify the development experiencehandling JS syntax conversion, CSS preprocessing, and integration of various tools (including linting, formatting, testing frameworks, etc.) -such as Webpack, Vite, Parcel, etc.</p><p>• Fullstack Framework: Fullstack usually refers to the ability of developers to handle both front-end (such as UI) and back-end (such as data and logic processing) development. In this context, it refers to frameworks that support both front-end and back-end development, such as Next.js, Nuxt, Express.js, etc.</p><p>• Database Framework Data management is a relatively independent and core part of back-end logic. Data can be managed directly via SQL using tools like SQLite, or through object-relational mapping frameworks such as Prisma and Sequelize.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head n=\"2.2.3\">Calibrate Project</head><p>Use Web-Bench evaluation tool to Calibrate a project. More details can be found in Appendix D. That is, check for design issues in the project:</p><p>1. Task issues, such as ambiguous descriptions, vulnerabilities, or obvious conflicts with testcases.</p><p>2. Testcase issues, such as not rigorous enough or too strict.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head n=\"3.\">Benchmark Evaluation</head><p>3.1 Project Structure src / # final solution codes index . html src -init / # initial solution codes index . html test / init . spec . js # cases for initial codes task -1. spec . js # cases for task -1 ... task -20. spec . js # cases for task -20 tasks . jsonl # support json and yaml 1. Get Initial Codes: optional step, get initial codes from agent. 2. Iterate Tasks: from task-1 to task-20. 3. Call Agent: more details about agent can be found in Section Web-Agent Workflow. 4. Rewrite files: update files or create new files. 5. Init Env: optional step,init files running environment. 6. Build Files: optional step, check files errors, such as reference errors. 7. Test: end-to-end (E2E) test with Playwright. 8. Retry: with error context from Build or Test.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head n=\"3.2\">Evaluator Workflow</head><p>(a) First attempt, if failed, implement task-n with task description and error context, goto step-3.</p><p>(b) Second attempt, if failed, evaluation is done.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head n=\"9.\">Report.</head><p>More details can be found in Appendix C.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head n=\"3.3\">Web-Agent Workflow</head><p>Web-Agent is an instance of the agent described in Section 3.2, Step 3. Web-Agent is the interaction module between Web-Bench and LLM. Its main responsibilities are to build prompts, request LLM API, and extract files from LLM response. 1. Build Prompts: Using system prompt (SP), task description, files, and error messages. If the combined input exceeds the context length, it will be truncated. 2. Request LLM: As shown in Table 2. Requests the LLM's OpenAPI based on the selected model and returns the response. In this step, parameters such as temperature, maxToken, contextLength are obtained from the model provider. 3. Extract Files: Parses the LLM's response to extract the generated files.</p><p>More details can be found in Appendix B.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>3.4</head><p>Metrics</p><formula xml:id=\"formula_0\">pass@1 = pass_tasks@1 tasks × 100% (<label>1</label></formula><formula xml:id=\"formula_1\">)</formula><p>• Percentage of tasks passing all E2E tests on the first attempt.</p><p>• pass_tasks@1: number of tasks passed on first attempt before any failure.</p><p>• tasks: total number of tasks in 'tasks.jsonl'. • pass_tasks@2: total number of passed tasks including retries.</p><formula xml:id=\"formula_2\">pass@2 = pass_tasks@2 tasks × 100%<label>(2)</label></formula><p>Example:</p><p>• tasks: 20</p><p>• pass_tasks@1: 5, the first failed evaluation happened at task-6</p><p>• pass_tasks@2: 15, total passed tasks is 15 (task-16's second attempt failed)</p><p>• pass@1: 5/20 × 100% = 25%</p><p>• pass@2: 15/20 × 100% = 75%</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head n=\"4.\">Results</head></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head n=\"4.1\">Evaluation Results</head><p>As shown in Figure <ref type=\"figure\" target=\"#fig_5\">7</ref>, 'claude-3-7-sonnet-20250219thinking' achieves the highest Pass@1 and Pass@2 (using Best-of-5 sampling, as used throughout). From the Pass@1 distribution, Claude-series models exhibit the highest performance, followed by GPT, Doubao, DeepSeek, LLAMA, Gemini, Qwen, and others. From the Pass@2 distribution, Claude-series models exhibit the highest performance, followed by Doubao, DeepSeek, GPT, Gemini, LLAMA, Mistral, and others.</p><p>As shown in Figure <ref type=\"figure\" target=\"#fig_5\">7</ref> and 8, regardless of Pass@1 or Pass@2, closed-source models (shown in red) also appear to have an advantage. As shown in Figure <ref type=\"figure\">9</ref>, the average Pass@2 of closed models is 20.79%, while that of open models is 14.84%. The average Pass@1 of closed models is 15.08%, while that of open models is 10.73%.</p><p>More details can be found in Appendix E.</p><p>As shown in Figure <ref type=\"figure\">10</ref> and 11, the Pass@2 distribution across LLM series indicates that newer or largerparameter models generally perform better in Web-Bench, which aligns with the commonly observed scaling law.   Figure 10: Pass@2 across LLMs within the same model series -Part 1 Figure 11: Pass@2 across LLMs within the same model series -Part 2</p><p>As shown in Figure <ref type=\"figure\" target=\"#fig_8\">12</ref>, LLMs of the same specification perform better on Pass@2 when the thinking mode is enabled. 4.2 Compare SOTAs As shown in Table <ref type=\"table\" target=\"#tab_2\">3</ref> and Figure <ref type=\"figure\" target=\"#fig_9\">13</ref>, HumanEval and MBPP have approached saturation. APPS and EvalPlus are approaching saturation. The SOTA for Web-Bench is 25.1%, which is lower (better) than that of the SWE-bench Full and Verified sets. </p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head n=\"5.\">Related Work</head><p>Early LLM code benchmarks focused on the accuracy of single-function code generation. We review several widely cited benchmarks:</p><p>• HumanEval <ref type=\"bibr\">[6]</ref>: 164 handwritten Python coding questions are used to evaluate functional correctness. Each question contains a function signature, docstring, and function body, and has an average of 7.7 test cases.</p><p>• MBPP <ref type=\"bibr\">[8]</ref>: About 1,000 entry-level crowd-sourced Python coding questions covering coding basics and standard library functions, with each question having an average of 3 test cases.</p><p>• APPS <ref type=\"bibr\" target=\"#b28\">[29]</ref>: 10,000 Python coding questions-5,000 for training and 5,000 for testing-sourced from multiple coding competition websites, covering simple problems, technical interview questions, and algorithm competition-level challenges. Each question has an average of 13 test cases. From Table <ref type=\"table\" target=\"#tab_2\">3</ref>, we can see that HumanEval is saturated, and MBPP is close to saturation. APPS performs slightly better due to its larger dataset size, but it is also approaching saturation. To address code benchmark saturation, many new ideas have emerged.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head n=\"5.1\">Increase programming languages</head><p>Most of the problems in early code benchmarks used Python as the programming language. In addition to Python, mainstream languages in real-world development include C++, JavaScript, Java, Go, etc., making it natural to consider expanding language coverage.</p><p>• MultiPL-E <ref type=\"bibr\">[10]</ref> translates HumanEval and MBPP to 18 other programming languages.</p><p>• HumanEval-X <ref type=\"bibr\">[11]</ref> extends the HumanEval benchmark to Python, C++, Java, JavaScript, and Go via human translations</p><p>• HumanEvalPack <ref type=\"bibr\" target=\"#b11\">[12]</ref> extends the HumanEval benchmark to cover three coding tasks-code repair, code interpretation, and code generation-and six languages (Python, JavaScript, Java, Go, C++, Rust) via human translations. As shown in Table <ref type=\"table\" target=\"#tab_2\">3</ref>, the SOTA in 2024 has reached 78%.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head n=\"5.2\">Increase test coverage</head><p>This stems from a simple idea: improving test coverage and increasing the presence of edge cases is expected to reduce the pass rate.</p><p>• CodeContests <ref type=\"bibr\">[13]</ref> is a competitive coding dataset for machine learning. It automatically generates additional test cases (mutations), such as bit flipping of input strings or integer addition and subtraction. It uses multiple correct solutions to verify the new tests and ensure consistent output, and filters out problems with insufficient or overly simple test cases.</p><p>• EvalPlus <ref type=\"bibr\" target=\"#b13\">[14]</ref> detects the robustness of generated code by strengthening the scope and number of HumanEval and MBPP tests. For the same LLM, Pass@1 can generally be reduced by 5-10%. However, as shown in Table <ref type=\"table\" target=\"#tab_2\">3</ref>, the latest SOTA has reached 89%, gradually approaching saturation.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head n=\"5.3\">Increasing dataset complexity</head><p>The problems in the early code benchmarks contain many basic algorithms, which are no longer challenging for LLMs; increasing the complexity of a single problem is also an idea.</p><p>• DS-1000 <ref type=\"bibr\">[15]</ref> is a code generation benchmark containing 1,000 data science problems, covering seven Python libraries such as NumPy and Pandas. The problems come from Stackoverflow and have been lightly edited.</p><p>• CoderEval <ref type=\"bibr\" target=\"#b15\">[16]</ref> contains 230 Python and 230 Java code generation problems carefully selected from popular open source projects. It supports code generation problems with six different levels of context dependency. It supports problems with six levels of context dependency, and performance is significantly better when generating independent functions compared to non-independent ones.</p><p>• BigCodeBench <ref type=\"bibr\" target=\"#b16\">[17]</ref> combines tools from 139 libraries across 7 domains to support 1,140 finegrained tasks. It integrates multiple existing benchmarks and enables the implementation of functions such as data analysis and web development by calling multiple tools.</p><p>• MLE-bench <ref type=\"bibr\" target=\"#b17\">[18]</ref> is a benchmark for measuring the performance of AI agents in machine learning engineering tasks. 75 ML engineering competitions were selected from Kaggle to evaluate real-world skills such as model training, dataset preparation, and running experiments for machine learning workflows. As shown in Table <ref type=\"table\" target=\"#tab_2\">3</ref>, the current SOTA is only 16.9%. As the coding capabilities of LLMs continue to improve, such professionally oriented benchmarks are expected to become more prevalent.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head n=\"5.4\">Improving software engineering reproducibility</head><p>Some benchmarks simulate the software engineering process from different perspectives:</p><p>• CodeXGLUE <ref type=\"bibr\" target=\"#b18\">[19]</ref> includes 14 datasets for 10 diverse programming language tasks including codecode, text-code, code-text and text-text scenarios. Provides three baseline models to support these tasks, including a BERT-style pre-trained model for understanding tasks, a GPT-style pretrained model for code completion and generation, and an encoder-decoder framework that supports sequence-to-sequence generation.</p><p>• RepoBench <ref type=\"bibr\" target=\"#b19\">[20]</ref> is a new benchmark containing three subtasks (RepoBench-R retrieval, RepoBench-C completion, RepoBench-P pipeline), supporting Python and Java, and covering the evaluation of automatic code completion systems at the code repository level.</p><p>• DevBench <ref type=\"bibr\" target=\"#b20\">[21]</ref> is based on the waterfall model. It explores the performance of the evaluated model across five stages: software design, environment configuration, implementation, acceptance testing, and unit testing.</p><p>• SWE-bench <ref type=\"bibr\" target=\"#b21\">[22]</ref> focuses on solving real and complex software engineering problems. 2,294 tasks come from 12 popular Python libraries on GitHub.</p><p>The filtering rules are as follows: approximately 90,000 Pull Requests were collected, each associated with a code base. A GitHub issue was resolved, the test files in the repository were modified, at least one test case was included, and the test status changed from failed to passed. SWEbench offers substantial scale and real-world software engineering complexity. In the past two years, it has gradually become a highly cited code benchmark. As shown in Table <ref type=\"table\" target=\"#tab_2\">3</ref>, after two years, the SOTA-especially on the Full set-remains far from saturation.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head n=\"6.\">Discussion</head></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head n=\"6.1\">Why Software Engineering?</head><p>Based on the discussion in Chapter 5 and the SOTA data presented in Table <ref type=\"table\" target=\"#tab_2\">3</ref>:</p><p>Increasing the types of programming languages cannot solve the benchmark saturation problem. This is intuitive. Real-world programming languages have their own characteristics and strengths, but are similar in expressiveness. In fact, LLM pass rates across different language versions of the same benchmark do not vary significantly <ref type=\"bibr\" target=\"#b11\">[12]</ref>.</p><p>Increasing test coverage seems to have some effect, but EvalPlus <ref type=\"bibr\" target=\"#b13\">[14]</ref> also approached saturation within two years of its publication. Although CodeContests <ref type=\"bibr\">[13]</ref> performs better, its unsaturated SOTA is likely due to the high difficulty of its competition-level question set. Similarly, in real-world scenarios, project complexity arises from factors such as lines of code, code logic complexity, and the structure of the module/component dependency tree. Even 100% test coverage cannot change the complexity of the project itself.</p><p>Increasing the complexity of the dataset-whether by increasing the complexity of individual problems <ref type=\"bibr\" target=\"#b15\">[16,</ref><ref type=\"bibr\" target=\"#b17\">18]</ref> or by expanding the scale of the benchmark <ref type=\"bibr\" target=\"#b16\">[17]</ref>-appears effective based on SOTA results. However, the upper limit of this approach often consists of uncommon and complex algorithmic problems, which gradually deviate from real-world coding. This may lead LLM coding to evolve in a less practical direction.</p><p>Simulating the software engineering process has been theoretically demonstrated as feasible by CodeXGLUE, RepoBench, and DevBench. These benchmarks have also been preliminarily validated on earlier LLMs, paving the way for larger-scale software engineering benchmarks such as SWE-bench and Web-Bench. Benchmarks in the software engineering direction are grounded in real-world coding behaviors.</p><p>Web-Bench is a new software engineering benchmark and fills the gap: Sequential task evaluation for fullstack web development based on Web standards and frameworks. 4</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head n=\"6.2\">LLMs require optimization tailored to Standards and Frameworks</head><p>Referring to the previous section, we note that software engineering provides a promising solution to benchmark saturation. Furthermore, if the problems contained in the benchmark (or in the projects presented in this work) accurately reflect real-world code, they can help guide LLMs to evolve in a practical direction. A fundamental question arises: what is the basis of real-world coding? Although this article primarily discusses web application development, the development process in any field <ref type=\"bibr\" target=\"#b29\">[30]</ref> is fundamentally built upon standards (also referred to as protocols, specifications, or recommendations) and frameworks (which may include libraries or third-party packages). Standards and Frameworks represent the basic knowledge and efficiency tools in a coding field, respectively. From this perspective, they are universally applicable. So we have an intuition: LLM needs optimization tailored to Standards and Frameworks.</p><p>We are also conducting further in-depth research in this direction, which may serve as a potential breakthrough in the next stage:</p><p>1. Standards-aware Training:</p><p>• Inject W3C/WHATWG documentation into pretraining data.</p><p>• Finetune with AST based syntax constraint checks.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head n=\"2.\">Framework-specific Adaptation:</head><p>• Add framework rule checkers to decoding loops.</p><p>• Develop plugin architectures for framework updates (avoiding retraining). This paper mainly studies the accuracy of code generation for LLMs. We have also received feedback from users on multiple occasions expressing interest in evaluating the aesthetics and code quality of the generated pages. Since the evaluation of aesthetics and code quality can largely reuse the existing benchmark infrastructure, Web-Bench already includes a built-in screenshot function. In the future, it can be easily extended to support additional metrics. </p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>Utility</head><p>Calculator-files</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>Other -Multiple-files version of Calculator project. Utility</head></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>Expression Editor</head><p>Other -A basic expression editor with features like: parenthesis auto closing, syntax highlighting, lint, autocompletion and more.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>Sheet</head></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>A. Appendix: Projects</head></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>A.1 Overview</head><p>See details in Table <ref type=\"table\" target=\"#tab_6\">5 6 7</ref>.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>A.1.1 UI Framework</head><p>UI frameworks offer component-based development, which can improve the efficiency of developing complicated web frontend application.</p><p>We have four projects: React, Vue, Angular, and Svelte, which represent four popular UI frameworks. These projects share the same tasks, and here is the pass@2 (Best of Five) for these projects:</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>Model</head></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>React Vue Angular Svelte</head><p>Claude-3.</p><p>7-T<ref type=\"foot\" target=\"#foot_1\">foot_1</ref> 60 40 50 55 Claude-3.7<ref type=\"foot\" target=\"#foot_2\">foot_2</ref> 65 30 40 25 Doubao-1.5-T<ref type=\"foot\" target=\"#foot_3\">foot_3</ref> 50 40 25 40 Doubao-1.5<ref type=\"foot\" target=\"#foot_4\">foot_4</ref> 35 35 5 10 GPT-4o 35 30 5 20 DeepSeek-R1 40 30 30 40 Analysis The following presents an analysis of these evaluation results: 1. When compared to Vue (208k stars), Angular (96k stars), and Svelte (79k stars), React (235k stars) is the most widely-used UI framework in the web domain and provides the largest dataset for LLM training. 2. React supports the JSX language feature, which combines JavaScript and HTML, thereby enhancing data density. 3. Vue/Svelte exhibits similarities to HTML grammar and demonstrates superior performance in certain models. 4. Angular features the most complex grammar, making it difficult for LLMs to learn and utilize.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>A.1.2 State Management</head><p>State management framework provides a structured and efficient approach for managing state changes.</p><p>We have four projects: Redux, Mobx, Zustand, and Jotai, which represent four popular state management frameworks. These projects share the same tasks, and here is the pass@2 (Best of Five) for these projects:</p><p>Model Redux Mobx Zustand Jotai Claude-3.7-T 45 50 55 45 Claude-3.7 45 50 45 40 Doubao-1.5-T 50 50 50 20 Doubao-1.5 35 15 35 20 GPT-4o 30 30 35 20 DeepSeek-R1 45 40 55 40</p><p>Analysis The following presents an analysis of these evaluation results: 1. Zustand demonstrates superior performance compared to other state-management frameworks, which can be attributed to its popularity and simple API design.</p><p>2. Mobx is easy to use, but also easy to make mistakes. For example, Mobx users may forget to add the observer higher-order component (HOC).</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head n=\"3.\">Zustand, Jotai, Mobx features a clean and straightforward API design.</head></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>A.1.3 Fullstack Framework</head><p>Fullstack Framework empowers developers to construct both the front-end and back-end components of a web application.</p><p>There are five projects available to demonstrate the large language model's (LLM) ability to implement fullstack applications. These projects encompass Nextjs, Nuxt, Expressjs, Fastify, and FastifyReact. These projects share the same tasks, and here is the pass@2 (Best of Five) for these projects:</p><formula xml:id=\"formula_3\">Model Next Nuxt</formula><p>Express Fastify F * 12<ref type=\"foot\" target=\"#foot_5\">foot_5</ref> Claude-3.7-T 35 25 40 40 35 Claude-3.7 40 25 45 40 35 Doubao-1.5-T 20 20 35 45 35 Doubao-1.5 15 20 50 15 5 GPT-4o 10 25 40 20 5 DeepSeek-R1 20 25 25 20 25 Analysis The following presents an analysis of these evaluation results: 1. Expressjs has the longest history (since 2010) in the fullstack domain, and its API is clean and stable across versions. 2. Although Nextjs has the highest number of stars, its API is not stable across different versions, and large language models (LLMs) may be confused by the differences between Server Components and Client Components. 3. When comparing Next and Nuxt: React-based Next is better than Vue-based Nuxt. 4. Fastify-React exhibits poor performance in simple tasks: React Router v7 in Fastify-React is a breaking change version update compared with v6, and in this case, react-router-dom is not required, yet LLMs often overlook this fact.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>A.1.4 Database</head><p>The database is crucial for the backend logic in a fullstack application. Developers can access the database through various Object-Relational Mapping (ORM) tools or Database APIs.</p><p>We present four projects (Sequelize, NoSQL, Prisma, and Lowdb) to showcase the Large Language Model's (LLM) capability to utilize ORM/Database APIs. These projects share the same tasks, and here is the pass@2 (Best of Five) for these projects:</p><p>Model Sequelize NoSQL Prisma Lowdb Claude-3.7-T 40 35 10 35 Claude-3.7 40 40 45 50 Doubao-1.5-T 20 35 25 30 Doubao-1.5 25 20 25 30 GPT-4o 20 25 5 25 DeepSeek-R1 40 35 20 30 Analysis The following presents an analysis of these evaluation results: 1. Nosql exhibits good performance. The Document Model of MongoDB is significantly easier to use than that of relation-based databases, and it is also suitable for Node.js. 2. Lowdb is an extremely simple JSON database. With lowdb, database APIs are the same as JavaScript Array APIs, which is simple enough for LLM to understand.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>A.2 Flex</head><p>Definition from <ref type=\"url\" target=\"https://www.w3.org/TR/css-flexbox-1/#abstract\">W3C:</ref> </p><p>The specification describes a CSS box model optimized for user interface design. In the flex layout model, the children of a flex container can be laid out in any direction, and can \"flex\" their sizes, either growing to fill unused space or shrinking to avoid overflowing the parent. Both horizontal and vertical alignment of the children can be easily manipulated. Nesting of these boxes (horizontal inside vertical, or vertical inside horizontal) can be used to build layouts in two dimensions.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>from MDN:</head><p>The CSS flexible box layout module defines a CSS box model optimized for user interface design, and the layout of items in one dimension. In the flex layout model, the children of a flex container can be laid out in any direction, and can \"flex\" their sizes, either growing to fill unused space or shrinking to avoid overflowing the parent. Both horizontal and vertical alignment of the children can be easily manipulated.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>Feature Coverage</head><p>This table shows the features used in solution codes ('src/') of the project. This is roughly the feature set that LLM will cover. Appendix A unless otherwise specified, the subsequent chapters have the same meaning.</p><p>Feature Feature display:flex ✓ column-gap ✓ flex-direction ✓ gap ✓ flex-wrap ✓ order ✓ flex-flow ✓ flex-grow ✓ justify-content ✓ flex-shrink ✓ align-items ✓ flex-basis ✓ align-content ✓ flex ✓ row-gap ✓ align-self ✓ A.3 Grid Definition from <ref type=\"url\" target=\"https://www.w3.org/TR/css-grid-1/#abstract\">W3C:</ref> This CSS module defines a two-dimensional grid-based layout system, optimized for user interface design. In the grid layout model, the children of a grid container can be positioned into arbitrary slots in a predefined flexible or fixed-size layout grid. from <ref type=\"url\" target=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn/CSS/CSS_layout/Grids\">MDN:</ref> CSS grid layout is a two-dimensional layout system for the web. It lets you organize content into rows and columns and offers many features to simplify the creation of complex layouts. Feature Coverage This table shows the features used in solution codes ('src/') of the project. Feature Feature display:grid ✓ grid-auto-rows ✓ grid-template-columns ✓ grid-column-end ✓ grid-template-rows ✓ grid-row-start ✓ grid-template-areas ✓ grid-row-end ✓ grid-template ✓ grid-column ✓ column-gap ✓ grid-row ✓ row-gap ✓ grid-area ✓ gap ✓ justify-self × justify-items ✓ align-self × align-items ✓ place-self × place-items ✓ order ✓ justify-content ✓ min-content ✓ align-content × max-content ✓ place-content ✓ fit-content() × grid-auto-columns × minmax() × grid-auto-flow ✓ min() ✓ grid ✓ max() ✓ grid-column-start ✓ subgrid × A.4 CSS Selector Definition from <ref type=\"url\" target=\"https://drafts.csswg.org/selectors/\">W3C:</ref> Selectors are patterns that match against elements in a tree, and as such form one of several technologies that can be used to select nodes in a document. They are a core component of CSS (Cascading Style Sheets), which uses Selectors to bind style properties to elements in the document. Project Design We a page with complex DOM structure to test different types of selectors and specificities. Tasks keywords: Task Task 1. Type/Class/ID Selector 11. Specificity 2. Compound Selector 12. Specificity 3. Selector list 13. Specificity 4. Combinator 14. Pseudo Class, Form 5. Combinator 15. Pseudo Class, Link 6. Pseudo Class 16. Nested Structure 7. Nesting Selector 17. :is() 8. Pseudo Class 18. :is() Deep Levels 9. Pseudo Class 19. :is() Deep Levels 10. Pseudo Element 20. :is() Deep Levels Feature Coverage This table shows the features used in solution codes ('src/') of the project. Feature Feature * ✓ :focus ✓ E ✓ :focus-within ✓ #id ✓ :enabled ✓ .class ✓ :disabled ✓ E.class1.class2 ✓ :default × sel1, sel2 ✓ :checked × E F ✓ :valid ✓ E &gt; F ✓ :invalid ✓ E + F ✓ :required ✓ E F ✓ :optional ✓ E[foo] ✓ :root × E[foo=\"bar\"] ✓ :empty ✓ E[foo=\"bar\"] ✓ :nth-child(n) ✓ E[foo=\"bar\"] ✓ :nth-last-child(n) ✓ E[foo$=\"bar\"] ✓ :only-child × E[foo*=\"bar\"] ✓ ::before ✓ E[foo|=\"bar\"] ✓ ::after ✓ :link ✓ :is() ✓ :visited ✓ :where() × :target ✓ :has() ✓ :active ✓ :not() ✓ :hover ✓ A.5 DOM Definition from <ref type=\"url\" target=\"https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#introduction-to-the-dom\">W3C:</ref> </p><p>In its original sense, \"The DOM\" is an API for accessing and manipulating documents (in particular, HTML and XML documents). Each such document is represented as a node tree. Some of the nodes in a tree can have children, while others are always leaves.</p><p>from <ref type=\"url\" target=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Document_Object_Model\">MDN:</ref> </p><p>The Document Object Model ( DOM ) connects web pages to scripts or programming languages by representing the structure of a document-such as the HTML representing a web page-in memory. Project Design We have developed a minimal File Explorer. In the project, there are 2 entry types including text file and directory. Typical File Explorer features are: Display Hierarchy Tree, Create, Delete, Rename, Modify (file only), Move, Copy and more. Tasks keywords:</p><p>1. Tools 2. Add File 3. Add Dir 4. SelectedEntry 5. Delete Entry 6. Drag and move 7. Context Menu , add / delete Entry , for . entry 8. Context Menu , add / delete Entry , for . entries 9. Context Menu , copy and paste 10. Context Menu , rename 11. Import 12. Export 13. Modify file content 14. Shortcuts , copy / paste / add 15. Shortcuts , delete 16. Generate large entry list 17. Resizer 18. Drag resizer 19. Filter 20. Context Menu , cut Feature Coverage This table shows the features used in solution codes ('src/') of the project. Figure 15: DOM Class Hierarchy (Partially) Feature Document.getElementById() ✓ Document.createElement() ✓ Document.createEvent() × ParentNode.querySelector() ✓ ParentNode.querySelectorAll() ✓ ParentNode.children ✓ EventTarget.addEventListener() ✓ EventTarget.removeEventListener() × Node.appendChild() ✓ Node.insertBefore() ✓ Node.removeChild() ✓ Node.replaceChild() ✓ Node.hasChildNodes() × Node.textContent ✓ Node.firstChild ✓ Node.lastChild ✓ Node.childNodes ✓ Node.parentNode ✓ Node.nextSibling ✓ Node.previousSibling ✓ Element.id ✓ Element.innerHTML ✓ Element.className ✓ Element.classList ✓ Element.clientHeight × Element.clientWidth × Element.scrollHeight × Element.scrollWidth × Element.getAttribute() ✓ Element.setAttribute() ✓ Element.hasAttribute() ✓ Element.removeAttribute() × Element.getElementsByClassName() × Element.getElementsByTagName() × Element.after() ✓ Element.before() × Element.remove() ✓ Element.closest() ✓ A.6 SVG Definition from <ref type=\"url\" target=\"https://svgwg.org/svg2-draft/Overview.html\">W3C:</ref> SVG is a language for describing twodimensional graphics. SVG drawings can be interactive and dynamic. Animations can be defined and triggered either declaratively (i.e., by embedding SVG animation elements in SVG content) or via scripting. from <ref type=\"url\" target=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/SVG\">MDN:</ref> Scalable Vector Graphics (SVG) is an XMLbased markup language for describing twodimensional based vector graphics. As such, it's a text-based, open Web standard for describing images that can be rendered cleanly at any size and are designed specifically to work well with other web standards including CSS, DOM, JavaScript, and SMIL. SVG is, essentially, to graphics what HTML is to text. Project Design We have developed a minimal draw application. Typical draw features are: 1. Shapes: Line, Rectangle, Ellipse, Circle, Polyline, Polygon, Path, Text 2. Common Property: LineWidth, Color 3. Operations: Move, Rotate, Zoom, Delete, Copy, Fill Task keywords: 1. Draw Line 2. Draw Rect 3. Draw Circle 4. Draw Ellipse 5. Delete Shape 6. Fill Shape 7. Copy Shape 8. Minimal Line and Rect 9. Minimal Circle and Ellipse 10. Draw Triangle 11. Draw Trapezoid 12. Draw Hexagon 13. Draw Curve ( Path ) 14. Draw Polyline 15. Draw Text 16. Move Shape 17. Shortcuts 18. Rotate Shape 19. Zoom Shape 20. Combined Action Feature Coverage This table shows the features used in solution codes ('src/') of the project.</p><p>Feature Feature</p><formula xml:id=\"formula_4\">Shapes &amp; Path - &lt;animate&gt; × &lt;rect&gt; ✓ &lt;animateMotion&gt; ✓ &lt;circle&gt; ✓ &lt;mpath&gt; ✓ &lt;ellipse&gt; ✓ &lt;animateTransform&gt; ✓ &lt;line&gt; ✓ &lt;set&gt; × &lt;polyline&gt; ✓ Styles - &lt;polygon&gt; ✓ &lt;linearGradient&gt; ✓ &lt;path&gt; ✓ &lt;radialGradient&gt; ✓ Text - &lt;pattern&gt; × &lt;text&gt; ✓ &lt;marker&gt; ✓ &lt;tspan&gt; × &lt;filter&gt; × &lt;textPath&gt; × &lt;clipPath&gt; ✓ Document Structure - &lt;mask&gt; ✓ &lt;svg&gt; ✓ color ✓ &lt;g&gt; ✓ transform ✓ &lt;defs&gt; ✓ transform-origin ✓ &lt;symbol&gt; ✓ fill ✓ &lt;use&gt; ✓ fill-rule × &lt;title&gt; ✓ fill-opacity ✓ &lt;desc&gt; × stroke ✓ &lt;switch&gt; × stroke-width ✓ &lt;a&gt; × stroke-linecap ✓ &lt;view&gt; × stroke-linejoin ✓ Embedded Content - stroke-miterlimit × &lt;image&gt; ✓ stroke-dasharray ✓ &lt;foreignObject&gt; ✓ stroke-dashoffset ✓ Animation (SMIL) - stroke-opacity ✓ A.7 React Definition from React:</formula><p>React is a JavaScript library for building user interfaces.</p><p>-Declarative: React makes it painless to create interactive UIs. Design simple views for each state in your application, and React will efficiently update and render just the right components when your data changes. Declarative views make your code more predictable, simpler to understand, and easier to debug.</p><p>-Component-Based: Build encapsulated components that manage their own state, then compose them to make complex UIs. Since component logic is written in JavaScript instead of templates, you can easily pass rich data through your app and keep the state out of the DOM.</p><p>-Learn Once, Write Anywhere: We don't make assumptions about the rest of your technology stack, so you can develop new features in React without rewriting existing code. React can also render on the server using Node and power mobile apps using React Native.</p><p>Project Design This Project create a blog system to test the ability of LLM to use React.js. Tasks is designed to verify Agent's knowledge of React ˙js:</p><p>1. Create components and use props 2. List Rendering 3. State &amp; Event 4. Visible -Control ( Modal ) 5. useEffect 6. Form Handling 7. React Context API 8. Custom Hook &amp; Delete in List State 9. Edit in List State &amp; Reuse of CreateForm 10. Filter List &amp; useMemo 11. Virtual List 12. u s e S y n c E x ternalStore 13. ReactPortal &amp; useRef ( Tooltip ) 14. XSS prevention 15. ReactDOM API ( showToast ) 16. TextOverflow 17. use forwardRef and useImperativeHandle 18. write a simple React Router 19. write a complete game 20. Communication between pages . Feature Coverage This table shows the features used in solution codes ('src/') of the project. Feature Feature Component ✓ useSyncExternalStore ✓ Props ✓ createPortal ✓ State ✓ dangerouslySetInnerHTML ✓ Event ✓ XSS Prevention ✓ List ✓ React DOM API ✓ Form ✓ startTransition × Custom Hook ✓ useDeferredValue × Context API ✓ forwardRef ✓ useEffect ✓ useImperativeHandle ✓ useRef ✓ lazy × useCallback ✓ memo × useMemo ✓ A.8 Threejs</p><p>Definition from <ref type=\"url\" target=\"https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js\">Threejs GitHub:</ref> </p><p>The aim of the project is to create an easyto-use, lightweight, cross-browser, generalpurpose 3D library. The current builds only include WebGL and WebGPU renderers but SVG and CSS3D renderers are also available as addons.</p><p>from <ref type=\"url\" target=\"https://threejs.org/manual/#en/fundamentals\">Threejs Manual:</ref> </p><p>Three.js is a 3D library that tries to make it as easy as possible to get 3D content on a webpage.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>Project Design</head><p>We have developed a 3D version of the Snake game by threejs. We have added a portal mechanism to this game. Typical Snake game tasks are:</p><p>1. Generate scene , Create a renderer and enable auto -refresh 2. Generate an 8 * 8 floor 3. Create a camera , Place a point light source 4. Generate a snake group and snakeHead 5. Generate snakeBody 6. Support movement control in forward , backward , left , and right directions 7. Generate a surrounding fence and add collision detection 8. Generate candy 9. Grow by one unit when the snake eats candy 10. Candy Animation 11. Generate portal 12. Portal animation 13. Candy cannot be generated in the portal 14. Mouse view rotation 15. Click 'h ' to center the view 16. View automatically follows the snake 's head movement 17. Snake resets to initial state when entering a dead -end 18. Snake auto movement 19. The snake changes color after passing through the portal 20. Game success when the snake 's length reaches 20</p><p>Feature Coverage This table shows the features used in solution codes ('src/') of the project.</p><p>Feature Feature</p><formula xml:id=\"formula_5\">Scenes ✓ Objects ✓ Cameras ✓ -BatchedMesh × -Camera ✓ -Bone × -CubeCamera × -Group ✓ -OrthographicCamera × -Line × -PerspectiveCamera ✓ -Mesh ✓ -StereoCamera × -Skeleton × Materials ✓ -Sprite × Geometry ✓ Light &amp; Shadow ✓ -BoxGeometry ✓ Loader × -CircleGeometry × Math ✓ -ConeGeometry ✓ Audio × -CylinderGeometry × Helper × -PlaneGeometry ✓ Animation ✓ -PolyhedronGeometry × -AnimationAction ✓ -RingGeometry ✓ -AnimationClip ✓ -ShapeGeometry × -AnimationMixer ✓ -SphereGeometry ✓ -AnimationObjectGroup × Textures × -</formula><p>AnimationUtils × Renderers ✓ A.9 Redux Definition from <ref type=\"url\" target=\"https://redux.js.org/introduction/getting-started\">Redux:</ref> Redux is a JS library for predictable and maintainable global state management. It helps you write applications that behave consistently, run in different environments (client, server, and native), and are easy to test. On top of that, it provides a great developer experience, such as live code editing combined with a time traveling debugger. Feature Coverage This table shows the features used in solution codes ('src/') of the project. Feature Feature Store ✓ createEntityAdapter × Actions ✓ Redux DevTools × Reducers ✓ Immutability ✓ Middleware ✓ React-Redux ✓ Combine Reducers ✓ connect × Async Actions ✓ useSelector ✓ Redux Toolkit ✓ useDispatch ✓ createSlice ✓ Provider ✓ configureStore ✓ Redux Thunk ✓ createAsyncThunk ✓ Redux Saga × createSelector × Redux Observable × A.10 Zustand Definition from <ref type=\"url\" target=\"https://zustand.docs.pmnd.rs/getting-started/introduction\">Zustand:</ref> A small, fast, and scalable bearbones state management solution. Zustand has a comfy API based on hooks. It isn't boilerplated or opinionated, but has enough convention to be explicit and flux-like. Feature Coverage This table shows the features used in solution codes ('src/') of the project. Feature Feature Store ✓ Immer Integration ✓ State Getters ✓ Immutability ✓ State Setters ✓ React Hooks ✓ Combine Stores × useStore ✓ Async Actions ✓ useShallow ✓ Selectors × DevTools × Computed Values ✓ Transient Updates × Persistence × Optics × A.11 Survey Project Design We have developed a minimal survey system including a survey designer and preview page. Task keywords are: Feature Feature Variables ✓ Operations ✓ -Map ✓ Functions ✓ -List × Inheritance × Mixins ✓ Comments ✓ Nesting ✓ Modules ✓ A.13 Vite Definition from <ref type=\"url\" target=\"https://vite.dev/guide/\">Vite:</ref> </p><p>Vite (French word for \"quick\", pronounced /vit/, like \"veet\") is a build tool that aims to provide a faster and leaner development experience for modern web projects. It consists of two major parts: -A dev server that provides rich feature enhancements over native ES modules, for example extremely fast Hot Module Replacement (HMR).</p><p>-A build command that bundles your code with Rollup, pre-configured to output highly optimized static assets for production.</p><p>Project Design Task Keywords:</p><p>1. Initialize project 2. Add path alias 3. Global variable replacement 4. SPA history fallback 5. Browser compatibility 6. Proxy 7. Using images 8. Using CSS / Less / CSS Modules 9. Using TypeScript 10. Using React / Vue 11. Hide sourcemap files 12. Remove all console . log call expressions 13. Extract licenses into standalone file 14. In -memory virtual module 15. Support mock in dev server 16. Compress images 17. Support importing compiled HTML from markdown files 18. Support importing frontmatter from markdown files 19. Handle assets resolving inside markdown files 20. Add i18n feature for markdown file Feature Coverage This table shows the features used in solution codes ('src/') of the project. Feature root × base ✓ mode × define ✓ plugins ✓ publicDir × cacheDir × resolve.alias ✓ resolve.dedupe × resolve.conditions × resolve.mainFields × resolve.extensions × resolve.preserveSymlinks × html.cspNonce × css.modules × css.postcss × css.preprocessorOptions × css.pre....additionalData × css.preprocessorMaxWorkers × css.devSourcemap × css.transformer × css.lightningcss × json.namedExports × json.stringify × esbuild × assetsInclude × logLevel × customLogger × clearScreen × envDir × envPrefix × appType × future × Feature Feature server.host ✓ server.hmr × server.allowedHosts × server.warmup × server.port ✓ server.watch × server.strictPort × server.middlewareMode × server.https × server.fs.strict × server.open × server.fs.allow × server.proxy ✓ server.fs.deny × server.cors × server.origin × server.headers × server.sourcemapIgnoreList × Feature build.target ✓ build.modulePreload × build.polyfillModulePreload × build.outDir ✓ build.assetsDir × build.assetsInlineLimit × build.cssCodeSplit × build.cssTarget × build.cssMinify × build.sourcemap ✓ build.rollupOptions ✓ build.commonjsOptions × build.dynamicImportVarsOptions × build.lib × build.manifest × build.ssrManifest × build.ssr × build.emitAssets × build.ssrEmitAssets × build.minify × build.terserOptions × build.write × build.emptyOutDir × build.copyPublicDir × build.reportCompressedSize × build.chunkSizeWarningLimit × build.watch × A.14 Nextjs Definition from <ref type=\"url\" target=\"https://nextjs.org/docs\">Nextjs:</ref> Next.js is a React framework for building full-stack web applications. You use React Components to build user interfaces, and Next.js for additional features and optimizations. Under the hood, Next.js also abstracts and automatically configures tooling needed for React, like bundling, compiling, and more. This allows you to focus on building your application instead of spending time with configuration. Whether you're an individual developer or part of a larger team, Next.js can help you build interactive, dynamic, and fast React applications. Project Design Tasks are designed to verify Agent's knowledge of Next.js: 1. Basic Router ( App Router or Page Router ) 2. Layout 3. NotFound + Link 4. Route Handler 5. List Page with Data Fetching from Sqlite 6. Add Dynamic Routes For Detail Page 7. Simple JWT Authentication &amp; Cookie 8. Login Form with Client Component 9. Register Form with Server Actions 10. Static User Profile with Redirect 11. User Widget with REALTIME STATUS ( Agent might forget it ) . Combine React Context Provider &amp; Server Actions . 12. ACCURATE Number Increment with Client Component &amp; Server Actions , update coin displayed STATIC User Profile 13. Add Admin Portals , add middleware to check privileges 14. Wish List 15. Cart Functionality 16. Place Order 17. Pay Order 18. Refunds Order 19. Comment System 20. Invitation System ( Bonus when new User Registered or Pay a New Order ) Feature Coverage This table shows the features used in solution codes ('src/') of the project. Feature Feature App Router ✓ ISR × Page Router ✓ Server Components ✓ Pages Router ✓ Client Components ✓ Layout ✓ Server Actions ✓ Route Handler ✓ getStaticProps × Redirect ✓ Cookie ✓ NotFoundPage ✓ getServerSideProps × API Routes ✓ Image Optimization × Dynamic Routes ✓ Dynamic Imports ✓ Route Groups × Middleware ✓ Parallel Routes × Loading UI × Intercepting Routes × Error Boundaries × Static Generation × generateMetadata × SSR ✓ Edge Runtime × A.15 Prisma Definition from <ref type=\"url\" target=\"https://www.prisma.io/docs/orm/overview/introduction/why-prisma\">Prisma:</ref> Prisma ORM's main goal is to make application developers more productive when working with databases. Here are a few examples of how Prisma ORM achieves this: -Thinking in objects instead of mapping relational data -Queries not classes to avoid complex model objects -Single source of truth for database and application models -Healthy constraints that prevent common pitfalls and anti-patterns -An abstraction that makes the right thing easy (\"pit of success\") -Type-safe database queries that can be validated at compile time -Less boilerplate so developers can focus on the important parts of their app -Auto-completion in code editors instead of needing to look up documentation Feature Coverage This table shows the features used in solution codes ('src/') of the project. Feature Schema Definition ✓ Model Creation (Prisma Client) ✓ Document Creation (create) ✓ Document Creation (createMany) ✓ Document Query (findMany) ✓ Document Query (findUnique) ✓ Document Update (update) ✓ Document Update (updateMany) ✓ Document Deletion (delete) ✓ Document Deletion (deleteMany) ✓ Relations (include) ✓ Middlewares × Computed Fields × Indexes × Validation × Aggregation × Transactions ($transaction) ✓ Extensions × Raw Database Access × Connection Management × Fluent API × Nested Writes × Query Filters × Instance Methods ×</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>B. Appendix: Web-Agent</head><p>Web-Agent Workflow is shown in Figure <ref type=\"figure\">6</ref>.</p><p>1. Build Prompts Using system prompt (SP), task description, files, and error messages. If the combined input exceeds the context length, it will be truncated.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>System Prompt</head><p>When generating new code : 1. Always produce a single code block . 2. Never separate the code into multiple code blocks . 3. Only include the code that is being added . 4. No explanation , no issue , only code . 5. Never omit any code . , 6. If the user submits a code block that contains a filename in the language specifier , always include the filename in any code block you generate based on that file . The filename should be on the next line as the language specifier in your code block . 7. Don 't repeat filename in code block . Always follow these guidelines when generating code responses . Task Description add divs ( class ' header ' , ' footer ' , ' content ') with arbitrary text in '. root ' element . '. root ' occupies total viewport and children elements together occupy total '. root ' space . header ( border -box ) is always fixed at the top of '. root '; footer ( borderbox ) is always fixed at the bottom of '. root '; content ( border -box ) occupies the remaining '. root ' space . USE tailwind grid only , NO flex , float and position , NO js .  Add new model for evaluation 1. For models deployed on OpenRouter, use the native OpenRouter provider with the following configuration: { \" title \" : \" anthropic / claude -3 -opus \" , \" provider \" : \" openrouter \" , \" model \" : \" anthropic / claude -3 -opus \" , \" apiBase \" : \" <ref type=\"url\" target=\"https://openrouter\">https :// openrouter</ref> . ai / api / v1 \" , \" apiKey \" : \" {{ OPENROUTER_API_KEY }} \" } 2. If existing providers do not meet your requirements, you can evaluate specific models by creating a new Provider. This is achieved by extending the BaseLLM:</p><formula xml:id=\"formula_6\">export</formula><p>abstract class BaseLLM { abstract provider : string abstract option : LLMOption info : Model abstract chat ( compiledMessages : ChatMessage [] , originOptions : CompletionOptions ) : Promise &lt;{ request : string error ?: string response : string } &gt; } 1. option -define parameters for LLM requests: export interface LLMOption { contextLength : number maxTokens : number temperature ?: number apiBase : string } 2. info -model metadata in apps/eval/src/model.json. 3. chat -custom request method that returns the generated text from the LLM. Use custom agent for evaluation Web-Bench supports custom agent by built-in HTTP-Agent. During the \"Call Agent\" step 3.2, the HTTP-Agent will: 1. Pass the Evaluator's context to your CustomAgent. 2. Return the CustomAgent's response to the Evaluator without modifications. The request and response formats can be found in the next section. C.2 C.2 Agent Data Format HTTP Request HTTP Request Method POST HTTP Request Body export interface AgentRequest { type : \" normal \" | \" init \" task : string // Code files , key is filePath , value is fileContent files ?: Record &lt; string , string &gt; // Error context error ?: string }</p></div><figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_0\"><head>Figure 2 :</head><label>2</label><figDesc>Figure 2: Distribution of projects across scenarios</figDesc><graphic coords=\"2,326.48,171.71,190.92,170.78\" type=\"bitmap\" /></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_1\"><head>Figure 3 :</head><label>3</label><figDesc>Figure 3: Distribution of projects across domains</figDesc><graphic coords=\"2,362.28,444.14,119.32,119.88\" type=\"bitmap\" /></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_2\"><head>Figure 4 :</head><label>4</label><figDesc>Figure 4: All Standards and Frameworks in Web-Bench</figDesc><graphic coords=\"3,54.00,54.00,238.65,212.84\" type=\"bitmap\" /></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_3\"><head>Figure 5 :</head><label>5</label><figDesc>Figure 5: Evaluator Workflow</figDesc><graphic coords=\"4,54.00,422.41,238.66,148.86\" type=\"bitmap\" /></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_4\"><head>Figure 6 : 2 .</head><label>62</label><figDesc>Figure 6: Web-Agent Workflow</figDesc><graphic coords=\"4,326.48,268.22,190.92,123.19\" type=\"bitmap\" /></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_5\"><head>Figure 7 :</head><label>7</label><figDesc>Figure 7: Pass@1 across all models.</figDesc><graphic coords=\"5,302.62,210.07,238.64,233.84\" type=\"bitmap\" /></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_6\"><head>Figure 8 :</head><label>8</label><figDesc>Figure 8: Pass@2 across all models.</figDesc><graphic coords=\"5,302.62,512.69,238.64,233.84\" type=\"bitmap\" /></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_7\"><head>Figure 9 :Figure 10 :Figure 11 :</head><label>91011</label><figDesc>Figure 9: Average Pass@1 and Pass@2 by model type (Open vs. Closed)</figDesc><graphic coords=\"6,302.62,101.98,238.64,59.96\" type=\"bitmap\" /></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_8\"><head>Figure 12 :</head><label>12</label><figDesc>Figure 12: Pass@2 across LLMs with and without thinking</figDesc><graphic coords=\"6,54.00,175.20,238.64,287.81\" type=\"bitmap\" /></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_9\"><head>Figure 13 :</head><label>13</label><figDesc>Figure 13: Benchmark SOTA Pass@1 as of April 2025</figDesc><graphic coords=\"6,302.62,323.86,238.63,111.66\" type=\"bitmap\" /></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_10\"><head>Figure 14 :</head><label>14</label><figDesc>Figure 14: DOM Tree &amp; HTML</figDesc><graphic coords=\"16,302.62,54.00,238.65,87.81\" type=\"bitmap\" /></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_11\"><head>Files</head><figDesc>html &gt; &lt; html lang = \" en \" &gt; &lt; head &gt; &lt; meta charset = \" UTF -8 \" / &gt; &lt; meta name = \" viewport \" content = \" width = device -width , initial -scale =1.0 \" / &gt; &lt; title &gt;Web -Bench Grid &lt;/ title &gt; &lt; link href = \" ./ assets / tailwind . css \" rel = \" stylesheet \" / &gt; &lt;/ head &gt; &lt; body class = \"m -0 \" &gt; &lt; div class = \" root grid grid -cols -[ auto_1fr_auto ] grid -rows -[ auto_1fr_auto ] h -screen \" &gt; &lt; div class = \" leftbar border -box bgblue -200 p -4 col -start -1 rowstart -1 row -span -3 w -64 \" &gt; Left Sidebar &lt;/ div &gt; &lt; div class = \" header border -box bggray -200 p -4 border -b bordergray -400 col -start -2 row -start -1 \" &gt; Header Content &lt;/ div &gt; &lt; div class = \" content border -box bggray -100 p -4 overflow -auto colstart -2 row -start -2 \" &gt; Main Content Area &lt;/ div &gt; &lt; div class = \" footer border -box bggray -200 p -4 border -t bordergray -400 col -start -2 row -start -3 \" &gt; Footer Content &lt;/ div &gt; &lt; div class = \" rightbar border -box bggreen -200 p -4 col -start -3 rowstart -1 row -span -3 w -64 \" &gt; Right Sidebar &lt;/ div &gt; &lt;/ div &gt; &lt;/ body &gt; &lt;/ html &gt; '''</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" type=\"table\" xml:id=\"tab_0\"><head>Table 1 :</head><label>1</label><figDesc>Average and maximum attributes of across 50 projects</figDesc><table><row><cell>Project</cell><cell cols=\"3\">Attribute Average Maximum</cell></row><row><cell>Solution</cell><cell>Files Lines</cell><cell>23.4 1947.9</cell><cell>76 6180</cell></row><row><cell>Testcases</cell><cell>PerTask Total</cell><cell>3.6 72.4</cell><cell>5 99</cell></row></table></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" type=\"table\" xml:id=\"tab_1\"><head>Table 2 :</head><label>2</label><figDesc>Model Parameters</figDesc><table><row><cell cols=\"4\">Model Vendor contextLength maxToken temperature</cell></row><row><cell>anthropic</cell><cell>50,000</cell><cell>8,192</cell><cell>1</cell></row><row><cell>qwen</cell><cell>10,000</cell><cell>4,096</cell><cell>default</cell></row><row><cell>deepseek</cell><cell>10,000</cell><cell>8,192</cell><cell>0</cell></row><row><cell>openai</cell><cell>10,000</cell><cell>8,192</cell><cell>0.4</cell></row><row><cell>doubao</cell><cell>10,000</cell><cell>4,096</cell><cell>default</cell></row><row><cell>openrouter</cell><cell>10,000</cell><cell>8,192</cell><cell>0.4</cell></row><row><cell cols=\"2\">• Percentage of tasks passing all E2E tests on the</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row><row><cell cols=\"2\">second attempt. pass@2 (retry with error context)</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row><row><cell cols=\"2\">is closer to the behavior of human engineers.</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row></table></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" type=\"table\" xml:id=\"tab_2\"><head>Table 3 :</head><label>3</label><figDesc>Benchmark SOTA Pass@1 as of April 2025</figDesc><table><row><cell>Benchmark</cell><cell>Pass@1</cell><cell>Date 3</cell><cell>SOTA</cell></row><row><cell>MLE-bench</cell><cell>16.9%</cell><cell>2024.10</cell><cell>AIDE o1-preview [18]</cell></row><row><cell>Web-Bench  *</cell><cell>25.1%</cell><cell>2025.4</cell><cell>Web-Agent (Claude 3.7 Sonnet)</cell></row><row><cell>SWE-bench Full</cell><cell>33.8%</cell><cell>2025.2</cell><cell>SWE-agent (Claude 3.7 Sonnet) [27]</cell></row><row><cell>CodeContests</cell><cell>34.7%</cell><cell>2024</cell><cell>LPW (GPT-4o) [13]</cell></row><row><cell>BigCodeBench</cell><cell>56.1%</cell><cell>2025.2</cell><cell>DeepSeek-V3</cell></row><row><cell>SWE-bench Verified</cell><cell>65.4%</cell><cell>2025.3</cell><cell>Augment Agent v0</cell></row><row><cell>HumanEvalPack</cell><cell>78.3%</cell><cell>2024.2</cell><cell>GPT-4</cell></row><row><cell>APPS</cell><cell>87.2%</cell><cell>2025.1</cell><cell>LPW (GPT-4o) [28]</cell></row><row><cell>EvalPlus</cell><cell>89.0%</cell><cell>2024.9</cell><cell>O1 Preview</cell></row><row><cell>MBPP</cell><cell>94.2%</cell><cell>2025.3</cell><cell>QualityFlow (Claude 3.5 Sonnet) [9]</cell></row><row><cell>HumanEval</cell><cell>99.4%</cell><cell>2024.6</cell><cell>LLMDebugger (OpenAI o1) [7]</cell></row></table></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" type=\"table\" xml:id=\"tab_3\"><head>Table 4 :</head><label>4</label><figDesc>Benchmarks Evolution</figDesc><table><row><cell>Benchmark Task Type</cell><cell cols=\"2\">Dependency Tech Stack</cell><cell>Evaluation Type</cell></row><row><cell>HumanEval Single Function</cell><cell>No</cell><cell>Python</cell><cell>Function Test</cell></row><row><cell cols=\"2\">SWE-bench Issue Fixing / Feature Low</cell><cell>Python</cell><cell>Unit Test</cell></row><row><cell>Web-Bench Project</cell><cell>Strong</cell><cell cols=\"2\">Web Fullstack E2E Test</cell></row><row><cell>7. Limitation</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row><row><cell cols=\"2\">Web 2.0 has brought about the rapid development of</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row><row><cell cols=\"2\">the Web development ecosystem. Dating back to 2005,</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row><row><cell cols=\"2\">nearly two decades have passed. Although the cur-</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row><row><cell cols=\"2\">rent version of Web-Bench covers the core Standards</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row><row><cell cols=\"2\">and Frameworks, many others remain uncovered due</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row><row><cell cols=\"2\">to workload constraints. In the future, we will strive</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row><row><cell cols=\"2\">to cover more standards and frameworks by innovating</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row><row><cell>project design and development tools.</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row></table></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" type=\"table\" xml:id=\"tab_4\"><head>Table 5 :</head><label>5</label><figDesc>Projects for Web Standardsrepresents tabular data that is information presented in a two-dimensional table comprised of rows and columns of cells containing data. It was used for layout in the early days of the Web, but now it is only recommended for displaying data.</figDesc><table><row><cell>Project</cell><cell>Domain</cell><cell>Description</cell></row></table><note><p>Utility BOM Javascript BOM (Browser Object Model) is the core part of JavaScript on the web. BOM contains many modern browser features, like Window, Location, History, Navigator, Timer, Dialog and more. Doc DOM Javascript DOM (Document Object Model) defines a platform-neutral model for events, aborting activities, and node trees. The main technology to manipulate web page elements. IDE DOM1 Javascript Single-file version of DOM project. The impact of the number of usercompared files and reference relationships on code generation for large models.</p><p>IDE</p><p>Form Javascript A form is a component of a web page that has form controls, such as text, buttons, checkboxes, range, or color picker controls. A user can interact with such a form, providing data that can then be sent to the server for further processing (e.g. returning the results of a search or calculation).</p><p>Survey</p><p>Draw Javascript A minimal draw application with typical basic shapes (Line, Ellipse, Rectangle) and actions (Move, Rotate, Zoom, Delete, Copy, Fill).</p><p>Graph</p><p>ChartBuilder Javascript A Chart Builder based on SVG-Chart Project, to generate a chart with user's config.</p><p>Chart</p></note></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" type=\"table\" xml:id=\"tab_5\"><head>Table 6 :</head><label>6</label><figDesc>Projects for Web Frameworks Part 1 with GitHub Stars collected in March 2025 user interfaces with components. A component is a piece of the user interface that has its own logic and appearance. A component can be as small as a button, or as large as an entire page. Vue, Angular are the similar frameworks. React has also become one of the preferred UI frameworks in software engineering practice.</figDesc><table><row><cell>Project</cell><cell>Domain</cell><cell>Stars/K Description</cell></row></table><note><p>A high-performance, low overhead web framework for Node.js. It's designed with performance in mind and is one of the fastest web frameworks available for Node.js.</p></note></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" type=\"table\" xml:id=\"tab_6\"><head>Table 7 :</head><label>7</label><figDesc>Projects for Web Frameworks Part 2 with GitHub Stars collected in March 2025</figDesc><table><row><cell>Project</cell><cell>Domain</cell><cell>Stars/K Description</cell></row></table></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" type=\"table\" xml:id=\"tab_7\"><head>Table 8 :</head><label>8</label><figDesc>Evaluator ConfigurationDifference between Local-Agent and HTTP-Agent -Local-Agent: Local-Agent has the basic capability to interact with LLM. It can specify the corresponding model in the 'apps/eval/src/model.json'. Local-Agent is actually the Web-Agent introduced by this paper B.-HTTP-Agent: Through HTTP-Agent, it calls the configured agentEndPoint to send a request to the custom Agent.</figDesc><table><row><cell>Parameter</cell><cell>Type</cell><cell cols=\"2\">Default Description</cell></row><row><cell>projects</cell><cell>string[]</cell><cell>[]</cell><cell>If empty, all projects will pass. Value is like \"web-</cell></row><row><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell>bench/calculator\".</cell></row><row><cell>agentMode</cell><cell>\"local\" | \"http\"</cell><cell>\"local\"</cell><cell></cell></row><row><cell cols=\"2\">agentEndPoint string</cell><cell>\"\"</cell><cell>When agentMode is set to \"http\", set http API for</cell></row><row><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell>network requests.</cell></row><row><cell>models</cell><cell>string[]</cell><cell>[]</cell><cell>'models' field in apps/eval/src/model.json</cell></row><row><cell>maxdop</cell><cell>number</cell><cell>30</cell><cell>max degree of parallelism</cell></row><row><cell>logLevel</cell><cell>\"info\" | \"warn\" |</cell><cell>\"info\"</cell><cell></cell></row><row><cell></cell><cell>\"debug\" | \"error\"</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row><row><cell>httpLimit</cell><cell>number</cell><cell>10</cell><cell>When agentMode is set to \"http\", maximum concur-</cell></row><row><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell>rent requests</cell></row><row><cell>projects</cell><cell>string[]</cell><cell>[]</cell><cell>If empty, all projects will pass. Value is like \"web-</cell></row><row><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell>bench/calculator\".</cell></row><row><cell>agentMode</cell><cell>\"local\" | \"http\"</cell><cell>\"local\"</cell><cell></cell></row><row><cell cols=\"2\">agentEndPoint string</cell><cell>\"\"</cell><cell>When agentMode is set to \"http\", set http API for</cell></row><row><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell>network requests.</cell></row></table></figure>\n\t\t\t<note xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" place=\"foot\" n=\"3\" xml:id=\"foot_0\"><p>Paper / Agent / LLM Publication Date</p></note>\n\t\t\t<note xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" place=\"foot\" n=\"4\" xml:id=\"foot_1\"><p>Claude3.7-T is claude-3-7-sonnet-20250219-thinking</p></note>\n\t\t\t<note xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" place=\"foot\" n=\"5\" xml:id=\"foot_2\"><p>Claude3.7 is claude-3-7-sonnet-20250219</p></note>\n\t\t\t<note xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" place=\"foot\" n=\"6\" xml:id=\"foot_3\"><p>Doubao1.5-T is doubao-pro-1.5-thinking</p></note>\n\t\t\t<note xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" place=\"foot\" n=\"7\" xml:id=\"foot_4\"><p>Doubao1.5 is doubao-pro-1.5-32k</p></note>\n\t\t\t<note xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" place=\"foot\" n=\"8\" xml:id=\"foot_5\"><p>F * 12 is FastifyReact.</p></note>\n\t\t</body>\n\t\t<back>\n\n\t\t\t<div type=\"acknowledgement\">\n<div><head>Acknowledgements</head><p>We thank <rs type=\"person\">YuBei Li</rs>, <rs type=\"person\">Quan Ding</rs>, <rs type=\"person\">Feng Gu</rs>, <rs type=\"person\">YuKun Wang</rs>, <rs type=\"person\">ChaoMo Li</rs>, <rs type=\"person\">Kai Shen</rs>, <rs type=\"person\">ShuLin Xin</rs>, <rs type=\"person\">LinJian Cao</rs>, and <rs type=\"person\">KaiGe Yu</rs> for helpful discussions and feedback on drafts of this work. We are also grateful to <rs type=\"person\">Seed Team</rs> for their work on software and hardware infrastructure used by this benchmark. We thank the <rs type=\"projectName\">Continue</rs> project for inspiring our web-agent, as well as all the open-source projects used in this work, including <rs type=\"affiliation\">Node, VSCode, RushStack, Vite, Playwright, React, Next</rs>.js, and more. Finally, we would like to thank all the organizations and individuals who contribute to web standards. Their work forms the foundation of the entire web development ecosystem, including but not limited to W3C, WHATWG, and <rs type=\"grantNumber\">TC39</rs>.</p></div>\n\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t<listOrg type=\"funding\">\n\t\t\t\t<org type=\"funded-project\" xml:id=\"_MFmMzZ3\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<idno type=\"grant-number\">TC39</idno>\n\t\t\t\t\t<orgName type=\"project\" subtype=\"full\">Continue</orgName>\n\t\t\t\t</org>\n\t\t\t</listOrg>\n\t\t\t<div type=\"annex\">\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>Error Prompt</head><p>Get the prompt that will be sent to LLM.</p><p>[ { \" role \" : \" system \" , \" content \" : \" \\ n # Rules \\ nWhen generating new code :\\ n1 . Always produce a single code block . ,2. Never separate the code into multiple code blocks . ,3. Only include the code that is being added . ,4. No explanation , no issue , only code . ,5. Never omit any code . ,6. If the user submits a code block that contains a filename in the language specifier , always include the filename in any code block you generate based on that file . The filename should be on the next line as the language specifier in your code block . ,7. Don ' t repeat filename in code block \\ n \\ nAlways follow these guidelines when generating code responses .\\ n \\ n # Example \\ n \\ nHere is an example of response :\\ n \\n &lt; example &gt;\\ n ''' html \\ nfile_a . html \\n &lt; div &gt; file_a &lt;/ div &gt;\\ n ' ' '\\ n ' ' ' typescript \\ nsub_dir / file_b . ts \\ nconsole . log (\\\" file_b \\\") \\n ' ' '\\n &lt;/ example &gt;\\ n \\ nHere are some error examples of response :\\ n \\ n1 . repeated filenames \\n &lt; example &gt;\\ n ' ' ' javascript \\ n index . js \\ nindex . js \\ n window . addEventListener ( ' DOMContentLoaded ' , () = &gt; {\\ n console . log ( ' Dark mode page loaded ') ;\\ n }) ;\\ n ' ' '\\n &lt;/ example &gt;\\ n \\ n2 . without filename \\n &lt; example &gt;\\ n ' ' ' javascript \\ n window . addEventListener ( ' DOMContentLoaded ' , () = &gt; {\\ n console . log ( ' Dark mode page loaded ') ;\\ n }) ;\\ n ' ' '\\n &lt;/ example &gt;\\ n \" } , { \" role \" : \" user \" , \" content \" : \" ''' index . html \\n &lt;! DOCTYPE html &gt;\\ n &lt; html lang =\\\" en \\\" &gt;\\ n &lt; head &gt;\\ n &lt; meta charset =\\\" UTF -8\\\" / &gt;\\ n &lt; meta name =\\\" viewport \\\" content =\\\" width = device -width , initial -scale =1.0\\\" / &gt;\\ n &lt; title &gt; Web -Bench Grid &lt;/ title &gt;\\ n &lt; link href =\\\"./ assets / tailwind . css \\\" rel =\\\" stylesheet \\\"/ &gt;\\ n &lt;/ head &gt;\\ n &lt; body class =\\\" m -0\\\" &gt;\\ n &lt; div class =\\\" root grid grid -rows -[ auto_1fr_auto ] h -screen \\\" &gt;\\ n &lt; div class</p><p>=\\\" header border -box bg -gray -200 p -4 border -b border -gray -400\\\" &gt; Header Content &lt;/ div &gt;\\ n &lt; div class =\\\" content border -box bg -gray -100 p -4 overflow -auto \\\" &gt; Main Content Area &lt;/ div &gt;\\ n &lt; div class =\\\" footer borderbox bg -gray -200 p -4 border -t bordergray -400\\\" &gt; Footer Content &lt;/ div &gt;\\ n &lt;/ div &gt;\\ n &lt;/ body &gt;\\ n &lt;/ html &gt;\\ n ' ' '\\ nadd divs ( class ' leftbar ' , ' rightbar ') with arbitrary text in '. root ' element . leftbar ( border -box ) is fixed at the left of '. root '; rightbar ( border -box ) is fixed at the right of '. root '; content occupies the remaining '. root ' space . USE tailwind grid only . \\ n Do not compress the original code in file and return full file .\\ nI only want the returned results to contain code , without any explanations . \" } ]</p><p>2. Request LLM As shown in Figure <ref type=\"figure\">2</ref>. Requests the LLM's OpenAPI based on the selected model and returns the response. In this step, parameters such as temperature, maxToken, contextLength are obtained from the model provider.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head n=\"3.\">Extract Files Parses the LLM's response to extract the generated files.</head><p>LLM response text is: ''' html index . html &lt;! DOCTYPE html &gt; &lt; html lang = \" en \" &gt; &lt; head &gt; &lt; meta charset = \" UTF -8 \" / &gt; &lt; meta name = \" viewport \" content = \" width = device -width , initial -scale =1.0 \" / &gt; &lt; title &gt;Web -Bench Grid &lt;/ title &gt; &lt; link href = \" ./ assets / tailwind . css \" rel = \" stylesheet \" / &gt; &lt;/ head &gt; &lt; body class = \"m -0 \" &gt; &lt; div class = \" root grid grid -rows -[ auto_1fr_auto ] grid -cols -[ auto_1fr_auto ] h -screen \" &gt; &lt; div class = \" leftbar border -box bggray -300 p -4 col -span -1 row -span -2 \" &gt; This is the leftbar &lt;/ div &gt; &lt; div class = \" header border -box bggray -200 p -4 col -span -2 row -span -1 sticky top -0 left -0 \" &gt; This is the header &lt;/ div &gt; &lt; div class = \" rightbar border -box bggray -300 p -4 col -span -1 row -span -2 \" &gt; This is the rightbar &lt;/ div &gt; &lt; div class = \" content border -box bggray -100 p -4 col -span -1 row -span -1 \" &gt; This is the content &lt;/ div &gt; &lt; div class = \" footer border -box bggray -200 p -4 col -span -3 row -span -1 sticky bottom -0 left -0 \" &gt; This is the footer &lt;/ div &gt; &lt;/ div &gt; &lt;/ body &gt; &lt;/ html &gt; '''</p><p>The generated result is: { \" files \" : { \" index . html \" : \" &lt;! DOCTYPE html &gt;\\ n &lt; html lang =\\\" en \\\" &gt;\\ n &lt; head &gt;\\ n &lt; meta charset =\\\" UTF -8\\\" / &gt;\\ n &lt; meta name =\\\" viewport \\\" content =\\\" width = devicewidth , initial -scale =1.0\\\" / &gt;\\ n &lt; title &gt; Web -Bench Grid &lt;/ title &gt;\\ n &lt; link href =\\\"./ assets / tailwind . css \\\" rel =\\\" stylesheet \\\"/ &gt;\\ n &lt;/ head &gt;\\ n &lt; body class =\\\" m -0\\\" &gt;\\ n &lt; div class =\\\" root grid grid -rows -[ auto_1fr_auto ] grid -cols -[ auto_1fr_auto ] h -screen \\\" &gt;\\ n &lt; div class =\\\" leftbar border -box bg -gray -300 p -4 col -span -1 row -span -2\\\" &gt; This is the leftbar &lt;/ div &gt;\\ n &lt; div class =\\\" header border -box bg -gray -200 p -4 col -span -2 row -span -1 sticky top -0 left -0\\\" &gt; This is the header &lt;/ div &gt;\\ n &lt; div class =\\\" rightbar borderbox bg -gray -300 p -4 col -span -1 rowspan -2\\\" &gt; This is the rightbar &lt;/ div &gt;\\ n &lt; div class =\\\" content border -box bg -gray -100 p -4 col -span -1 row -span -1\\\" &gt; This is the content &lt;/ div &gt;\\ n &lt; div class =\\\" footer border -box bg -gray -200 p -4 col -span -3 row -span -1 sticky bottom -0 left -0\\\" &gt; This is the footer &lt;/ div &gt;\\ n &lt;/ div &gt;\\ n &lt;/ body &gt;\\ n &lt;/ html &gt; \" } } C. Appendix: Evaluator Tools  <ref type=\"table\">8</ref>.</p><p>Use HTTP-Agent { \" agentMode \" : \" http \" , \" agentEndPoint \" : \" <ref type=\"url\" target=\"http://example\">http :// example</ref> . com / path / to / agent \" } Use Local-Agent It is actually the Web-Agent introduced by this paper B. { \" agentMode \" : \" local \" , \" models \" : [ \" claude -3 -5 -sonnet -20241022 \" , \" openai / gpt -4 o \" ] } and the file 'apps/eval/.env' must be created locally</p><formula xml:id=\"formula_7\">ANTHROPIC_API_KEY = xxx OPENROUTER_API_KEY = xxx # Comment # OPENAI_API_KEY = xxx</formula><p>'XXX_API_KEY' is used by 'apps/eval/src/model.json' { \" models \" : [ { \" model \" : \" claude -3 -5 -sonnet -20241022 \" , \" apiKey \" : \" {{ ANTHROPIC_API_KEY }} \" } , { \" model \" : \" openai / gpt -4 o \" , \" apiKey \" : \" {{ OPENROUTER_API_KEY }} \" } ] } 3. Build docker build -f ./ start . dockerfile -t webbench .</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>Usage docker run web -bench</head><p>The results will be saved in 'apps/eval/report'.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>Q &amp; A</head><note type=\"other\">HTTP Response HTTP</note><p>Response Status -200, Successful -500, Failed HTTP Response Body export interface AgentResponse { // Code files , key is filePath , value is fileContent files : Record &lt; string , string &gt; }</p><p>Example -Init Task Request -Method: POST -Body: { \" type \" : \" init \" , \" task \" : \" generate a calculator in a single HTML file . the first row should be an input element with id ' display '; the next 4 rows should contain buttons with digits from '0 ' to '9 ' and operators including '+ -*/=. '; the last row should have a ' Clear ' button .</p><p>display ' Error ' when catching exception or getting undefined value during calculating . And add the html file filename after code block . The filename should be on the next line as the language specifier in your code block . the filename is \\\" index . html \\\" \" } Response -Status: 200 -Body: { \" files \" : { \" index . html \" : \" &lt;! DOCTYPE html &gt;\\ n &lt; html lang =\\\" en \\\" &gt;\\ n &lt; head &gt;\\ n &lt; meta charset =\\\" UTF -8\\\" &gt;\\ n &lt; meta name =\\\" viewport \\\" content =\\\" width = device -width , initial -scale =1.0\\\" &gt;\\ n &lt; title &gt; Calculator &lt;/ title &gt;\\ n &lt; style &gt; body {}\\ n . calculator {} &lt;/ style &gt;\\ n &lt;/ head &gt;\\ n &lt; body &gt; &lt;/ body &gt;\\ n &lt;/ html &gt; \" } } Example -Normal Task with Error Request -Method: POST -Body:</p><p>-Note: All file contents of the project will be transferred. Benchmark projects are relatively simple at present (mostly single files, a few single-digit files), and in the future, when the project structure becomes more complex, it will support shared folders to reduce the amount of data transferred. { \" type \" : \" normal \" , \" task \" : \" add button sqrt with text ' sqrt ' at the right of button clear ; click it to calculate result using display content directly \" , \" files \" : { \" index . html \" : \" &lt;! DOCTYPE html &gt;\\ n &lt; html lang =\\\" en \\\" &gt;\\ n &lt; head &gt;\\ n &lt; meta charset =\\\" UTF -8\\\" &gt;\\ n &lt; meta name =\\\" viewport \\\" content =\\\" width = device -width , initial -scale =1.0\\\" &gt;\\ n &lt; title &gt; Calculator &lt;/ title &gt;\\ n &lt; style &gt; &lt;/ style &gt;\\ n &lt;/ head &gt;\\ n &lt; body &gt; &lt;/ body &gt;\\ n &lt;/ html &gt;\\ n \\ n \" , \" index . css \" : \" body { }\\ n \\ n . calculator { } \" , \" index . js \" : \" let displayValue = '' // more \" } , \" error \" : \" \\ t \\ t \\ t \\ t \\ tAn error occurred during the execution of the unit test . \" } Response -Status: 200 -Body: { \" files \" : { \" index . html \" : \" &lt;! DOCTYPE html &gt;\\ n &lt; html lang =\\\" en \\\" &gt;\\ n &lt; head &gt;\\ n &lt; meta charset =\\\" UTF -8\\\" &gt;\\ n &lt; meta name =\\\" viewport \\\" content =\\\" width = device -width , initial -scale =1.0\\\" &gt;\\ n &lt; title &gt; Calculator &lt;/ title &gt;\\ n &lt; style &gt; &lt;/ style &gt;\\ n &lt;/ head &gt;\\ n &lt; body &gt; &lt;/ body &gt;\\ n &lt;/ html &gt;\\ n \\ n \" , \" index . css \" : \" body {} \" , \" index . js \" : \" let displayValue = '' // more \" , } }</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>D. Appendix: Calibrate Project</head></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>D.1 Workflow</head><p>Use rush eval to Calibrate a project. That is, check for design issues in the project:</p><p>1. tasks.jsonl issues, such as ambiguous descriptions, vulnerabilities, or obvious conflicts with test cases.</p><p>2. test/ cases issues, such as not rigorous enough or too strict.</p><p>The current path is '/path/to/projects/dom'.</p><p>1. rush eval with 'config.json5'. { projects : [ ' @web -bench / selector '] , models : [ ' claude -3 -5 -sonnet -20241022 '] , } 2. IF: task-20 passed, end 3. ELSE: use eval project files to replace 'src/': mv src src -20 # your manual codes cp -r eval / eval -2024 xx / claude -3 -5 -xx / task -5 -2 src 4. IF: Modify 'src/' or test/ codes 1. IF: All tests passed in 'src/' and src-20/ 2. THEN: rush eval with new config.json5 { projects : [ ' @web -bench / selector '] , models : [ ' claude -3 -5 -sonnet -20241022 ' ] , startTask : ' task -6 ' , } 3. goto Step-2 5. IF: Modify tasks.jsonl, task-n is the smallest index of modified tasks 1. THEN: use successful task-n-1 eval project files to replace 'src/': rm -rf src cp -r eval / eval -2024 xx / claude -3 -5 -xx / task -{ n -1} src 2. THEN:rush eval with new config.json5 { packageNames : [ ' @web -bench / selector '] , models : [ ' claude -3 -5 -sonnet -20241022 ' ] , startTask : ' task -n ' , } 3. goto Step 2 6. Create a doc from template D.2. D.2 Template Calibrate with 'claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022':</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>E. Appendix: Evaluation Models</head><p>See Table <ref type=\"table\" target=\"#tab_11\">9</ref>. </p></div><figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_12\"><head>C. 1</head><label>1</label><figDesc>